# .agentrules — SOC Home Lab

## Project Context

This is a cybersecurity SOC home lab project built with VirtualBox, Splunk SIEM,
Kali Linux, and Windows 10. It is a portfolio project for a CS undergraduate
targeting SOC Analyst and Security Engineer roles.

## Stack & Environment

- SIEM: Splunk Enterprise 10.2.3 on Ubuntu Server 26.04 (192.168.56.10)
- Attack Target: Windows 10 Pro (192.168.56.101)
- Attacker: Kali Linux (192.168.56.102)
- Network: VirtualBox Host-Only (192.168.56.0/24)
- Language: Python 3 for automation scripts (Phase 4)
- Query Language: SPL (Splunk Search Processing Language)

## Repo Structure

- docs/ — phase write-ups and cheatsheet
- splunk/queries/ — .spl detection rule files
- splunk/dashboards/ — exported dashboard XML
- splunk/alerts/ — alert configurations
- scripts/ — Python automation scripts (Phase 4)
- attack-scenarios/ — markdown write-ups per attack
- screenshots/ — evidence screenshots per phase

## Coding Rules

- Python scripts go in scripts/ only
- SPL queries go in splunk/queries/ as .spl files, one file per detection rule
- Never hardcode API keys or credentials — use config.py with environment variables
- All scripts must include a docstring explaining what the script does
- Follow PEP 8 for Python code style

## Documentation Rules

- Every phase has a corresponding markdown file in docs/
- Every detection rule must include a comment explaining what it detects and why
- Screenshots must be taken at every verification checkpoint and saved to screenshots/phaseN/
- Attack scenarios must be documented in attack-scenarios/ as markdown

## Git Rules

- Branch naming: phase-N-description (e.g. phase-3-sysmon)
- Commit message format: conventional commits
  - feat: new detection rule or script
  - fix: bug fix or config correction
  - docs: documentation updates
  - chore: repo maintenance
- Never commit credentials, API keys, or VM files
- Merge to main only when phase is fully verified and documented

## Detection Rule Rules

- Every SPL query must be tested in Splunk before committing
- Every detection rule must map to a MITRE ATT&CK technique ID
- Include the MITRE technique ID as a comment at the top of each .spl file
- Detection thresholds must be documented with reasoning

## What NOT to Do

- Do not modify VM configuration files directly — document changes in docs/
- Do not push .vdi, .vbox, or any VM disk files to GitHub
- Do not run attacks outside the Host-Only network (192.168.56.0/24)
- Do not skip verification checkpoints before moving to the next task
- Do not hardcode Splunk server IP in scripts — use config.py

## Phase Progress

- Phase 1: Complete — SIEM setup and log forwarding
- Phase 2: In Progress — attack simulation and detection rules
- Phase 3: Not started — Sysmon advanced logging
- Phase 4: Not started — Python automation and VirusTotal API
- Phase 5: Not started — Incident response (optional)

